# Check every text file out with LF, on every platform.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT IT.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf=true` — the default on a Windows install, and set in
# every clone made by `gh` — rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless
# for source you only compile, and it is not harmless here, because several of
# this project's guarantees are BYTE comparisons:
#
#   - tools/sync-guard.mjs asserts the shared engines copied into each skill
#     are byte-identical to each other.
#   - `route.mjs catalog --check` asserts the committed catalog matches the
#     one regenerated from the skills on disk.
#
# Measured on a fresh clone before this file existed: catalog.json arrived with
# 1,474 CRLF line endings and 58,914 bytes against the 57,440 that were
# committed — a 1,474-byte difference, one per line. The JSON parsed identically
# and every value matched. Only the bytes differed, so `--check` failed and the
# test suite reported three failures on a clean clone of a repository whose
# tests pass in the directory it was published from.
#
# That is the worst shape a bug can take here: it cannot be reproduced by the
# author, it greets every new user on their first `npm test`, and the failure
# message ("catalog.json does not match the skills on disk") points at the
# skills, which are fine.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files git must never touch.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.mp4           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
